This put us in perspective of how much raw data must be processed per run, that has to be processed. In order to schedule the processes consistently we have created a pipeline, which processes the traces, creates the cascades files per run `.tsv` (@apx-pipeline-cascades) and with those outputs generate the datasets @apx-pipeline-datasets. This is scheduled using the Zig build system by managing the programs execution as a make file (@apx-pipeline-build) in order to automatically ran them sequentially over the newly generated data. 

== Construct Cascades
<apx-pipeline-cascades>

Construct cascades is the `construct-cascade` Zig project (in `des-ctic/cascade`) that stitches the creation, propagation and repost traces into a single cascade per post.

The program does not read the `action.jsonl` shown in @code-action-sample ---that is the human-readable form of the action trace--- but its binary counterpart (see @sec-design-traces): `{run}-create_trace.bin`, `{run}-propagation_trace.bin` and `{run}-action_trace.bin`. From the action trace it keeps the `repost` decisions (dropping `ignore` and routing `like` to a separate file), and combines them with the creations and propagations into the `cascades.tsv` format shown in @code-cascades-sample.

