This project must be as performant as the simulation itself: a fast simulation feeding a slow data-processing pipeline is an equally annoying bottleneck. That is why this project is also written in Zig and uses several properties of the traces, such as being totally ordered, to reduce to just one read per file. Let's discuss the algorithm in depth.

=== Algorithm

The algorithm is split in two clean parts.

*Part 1*: the create, propagation and action traces together carry every event a cascade needs. The first step is to split them into `buckets` to allow for parallelization and to keep the input manageable. Every bucket should hold roughly the same number of posts, so the workload is split equally; each event is assigned to a bucket by hashing its `post_id` and taking the remainder modulo the number of buckets, as shown in @code-cascade-hash.

#code(caption: flex-caption([Bucket assignment by hashing the `post_id`.], [Bucket assignment by hashing the `post_id` with Wyhash and taking the remainder modulo the number of buckets. The `print` already writes the `tsv` line format.]))[
  ```zig
  const hashed_id = std.hash.Wyhash.hash(0, std.mem.asBytes(&pc.post_id));
  const bucket_id = hashed_id % bucket_writers.len;

  try bucket_writers[bucket_id].print(line_fmt, .{ id, pc.post_id, pc.user_id, -1, "propagation", pc.time });
  ```
] <code-cascade-hash>

As can also be seen in the `print` of @code-cascade-hash, the `tsv` output is already written in this first pass.

*Part 2.* Because each bucket has been split by `post_id`, two guarantees hold:

1. The whole cascade of a `post_id` is inside a single bucket.
2. Within each type (`creation`, `propagation`, `repost`) events are time-sorted, since every trace is written in time order.

From each bucket we then have to collect all the events of the same cascade (`run_id`, `post_id`), sort the cascades, and write them to the result file. To collect every cascade at once, the whole bucket is read into memory#footnote[Each bucket holds a small fraction of the total data, so it fits comfortably in memory.], and its lines are parsed into a `HashMap` keyed by a combined key, whose values are an `ArrayList` of events.

The clever part of the algorithm is the same bit-packing idea as in the Ziggurat implementation ---there, a single random word is split into two values (see @sec-method-rng-ziggurat)--- applied in reverse: the `run_id` and `post_id` are packed into a single word, as shown in @code-cascade-key.

#code(caption: flex-caption([Packing the cascade key.], [Packing `run_id` (high 32 bits) and `post_id` (low 32 bits) into a single `u64`, so sorting on the integer orders cascades by the `(run_id, post_id)` tuple.]))[
  ```zig
  const key = (@as(u64, parsed.run_id) << 32) | @as(u64, parsed.post_id);
  ```
] <code-cascade-key>

Once the map is built, the cascades are sorted by key with `std.mem.sort` @zig-std-sort#footnote[Which sorting algorithm `std.mem.sort` uses was not checked; since the data is mostly sorted, a custom merge could certainly save cycles there.], and each cascade's events are dumped, in order, into the output `cascades.tsv`. Likes are routed to a separate `cascades_likes.tsv` in the same pass.

=== Improvements

- Parallelization: especially noticeable in the 1M runs --- every bucket can be sorted and written independently.
- Parquet implementation: writing a TSV in step 1 and re-parsing it in step 2 is wasteful and slow; the same improvement the whole simulation could use.
- Sorting: knowing the data is mostly sorted within each type, there must be a faster way to merge them that does not require a full sort.

== Dataset Creation
<apx-pipeline-datasets>

Once the dataset `cascade.tsv` is created, we can now generate the analyzable `.parquet` datasets. There are 9 distinct datasets:

+ *Run metrics* (`run_metrics.parquet`) --- one row per run, with the global counters used to validate the simulation against its internal `SimMetrics`.
+ *Users* (`users.parquet`) --- one row per user and run, aggregating their sessions and actions.
+ *Sessions* (`sessions.parquet`) --- one row per session, with its timestamps, duration and per-session action counts.
+ *Post metrics* (`post_metrics.parquet`) --- one row per post, with its engagement counts and conversion rate.
+ *Raw post lifetime* (`raw_post_lifetime.parquet`) --- one row per repost, with propagation timing and inter-repost gaps; this feeds the next dataset.
+ *Post lifetime* (`post_lifetime.parquet`) --- one row per post, aggregating the raw dataset into $T_50$, $T_95$, $T_99$ and time-to-peak.
+ *Cascades* (`cascades.parquet`) --- one row per cascade, with tree metrics (depth, size, out-degree, structural virality).
+ *Broadcast groups* (`broadcast_groups.parquet`) --- one row per parent in a cascade, measuring how fast its audience picks up the content.
+ *Root-to-leaf paths* (`root_to_leaf.parquet`) --- one row per root-to-leaf chain, measuring end-to-end traversal.

Despite generating so many datasets, the both the Methodology and Results section (@sec-method and @sec-results respectively) do not report so many results. This is due to the necessary scope reduction the work suffered, as datasets (4), (5), (6), (8) and (9) are unused and not compared to the real data.

