== Creation

The creation of the dataset receives all data as an input: the four `.parquet`/`bin` traces, the cascades of reposts and likes, and the topology the simulation ran over. From there, the Go program does either of two things:
- for the six DuckDB datasets --- *run metrics*, *users*, *sessions*, *post metrics*, *raw post lifetime* and *post lifetime* --- it calls a preexisting `sql` script that generates the parquet;
- for the three cascade datasets --- *cascades*, *broadcast groups* and *root-to-leaf paths* --- it executes Go code to compute the structural virality and the subcascade metrics. 

Every code is relatively straightforward to look and check, and there are no architectural decisions other than using a CSR (see @apx-impl-csr) to represent the graph again, but this time in Go instead of Zig.

== Orchestration
<apx-pipeline-build>

With all pieces described, we have the following situation if we want to have usable datasets from the simulation:
- Make a run of the simulation, which will have $n$ iterations in $w$ workers, making every worker run a batch of $approx n/w$ iterations, as well as specifying the configuration and the topology as input.
- With the simulation traces as input, specifying the output directory --- and maybe changing the location or number of the buckets, although that is not very common.
- Lastly, provide all the outputs of both `bskysim` and `construct-cascade` to the dataset creation, together with the output directory, in order to obtain the results.

Additionally, all projects must be compiled for the target architecture in `ReleaseFast` mode (and its Go equivalent). As the reader can see, it is very time consuming to write all of this for a single run. This is what justifies the creation of a pipeline, or an orchestrator. Before attempting to write it in Python, despair became apparent, and I turned to the last amazing Zig feature this work will showcase: the build system.

The Zig Build System @zig-build-system is a program of the Zig standard library that allows building a Zig program in Zig. The closest well-known program that implements the same functionality is `make`, which compiles and manages C builds. The strong point of the Zig build system is that, being written in Zig itself, there is no need to learn another language in order to make use of it.

In all Zig projects used by this work, the build system has already been used. For example, to add all the dependencies in the simulation ---the `distribution`, `args` and `data-structures`, `set`--- as well as the `jemalloc` `c_allocator` was specified in the root `build.zig` file.
  
As it is not the objective of this section to explain how it was implemented nor how the Zig Build System works#footnote[An accessible introduction to its basics is available at @zig-build-system], we will describe what has been implemented.

First, all the arguments to all the programs can now be provided with a single JSON file, shown in @code-pipeline-config. The JSON must contain the `simulation`, `cascade` and `dataset` keys, each of which can be `null` to skip that stage.

#code(caption: flex-caption([Example pipeline configuration.], [Example pipeline configuration for a 10K run: `simulation` writes the traces, `cascade` builds the cascade TSVs from them, and `dataset` generates every dataset from both. A `null` section skips that stage; all paths are relative to the repository root.]))[
  ```json
  {
    "simulation": {
      "workers": 8,
      "runs": 100,
      "output_dir": "steps/traces/10K_run",
      "data_file": "data/monotonic/10K_monotonic.bin",
      "config_file": "job_config/prod.json"
    },
    "cascade": {
      "buckets": null,
      "bucket_file": null,
      "output_file": "steps/cascades/10K_run.tsv",
      "traces_dir": "steps/traces/10K_run"
    },
    "dataset": {
      "output_dir": "steps/datasets/10K_run",
      "cascades_ssv": "steps/cascades/10K_run.tsv",
      "likes_ssv": "steps/cascades/10K_run_likes.tsv",
      "traces_dir": "steps/traces/10K_run",
      "dataset": "all"
    }
  }
  ```
] <code-pipeline-config>

All the paths are relative to the root of the repository, where `build.zig` lives, so relative-path problems do not exist. This file must be provided with the option `-Dconfig="path/to/conf.json"`; otherwise the system will not build.

To make it more versatile, several subsets of the pipeline can be run independently:
- `zig build all`: runs the pipeline back-to-back.
- `zig build sim`: runs just the simulation.
- `zig build cascades`: runs the cascades.
- `zig build datasets`: runs the datasets.
- `zig build pipeline`: runs cascades and then datasets.

So if a process gets interrupted midway, we can pick it up with the build system directly.

Lastly, the Build System controls whether the code it calls needs compilation. If the project being called is another Zig project, the integration is tighter, but if it is not, the compilation is performed with a subprocess invocation, as shown in @code-build-compilation.

#code(caption: flex-caption([Compilation control in `build.zig`.], [How `build.zig` controls compilation: the Zig dependencies are declared as artifacts and compiled natively, while the Go binary is built through a `go build` subprocess.]))[
  ```zig
  const sim_exe = b.dependency(
    "bskysim",
    .{ .optimize = .ReleaseFast }
  ).artifact("bskysim");
  b.installArtifact(sim_exe);
  // cascades here, same procedure. skipped for brevity

  // The go binary must be compiled manually
  const go_build = b.addSystemCommand(
    &.{ "go", "build", "-o", "../bin/dataset-creation", "." }
  );
  go_build.setCwd(.{ .cwd_relative = "dataset" });
  b.getInstallStep().dependOn(&go_build.step);
  ```
] <code-build-compilation>
 
This allows us to control and reduce input errors when running the whole simulation, at the same time that being a pleasure to use; especially if one has had the opportunity to atempt a complex coordination stuff with make.
